1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,162
WWW.MY-SUBS.CO

﻿1
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:43,750
- Shall I help?
- Well, you can.

2
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:45,390
- You know what these
are, don't you? - No.

3
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:48,080
Pig boards. They control the pigs.

4
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:52,750
Are you annoyed we've given
Yew Tree Farm to Mr Mason?

5
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:55,550
I'm annoyed you fixed it
while I was in London,

6
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:56,990
but no, he's a good man.

7
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:58,910
And I hear pigs are his speciality.

8
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:01,100
No wonder you were convinced.

9
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:05,200
So now that you've settled in, have you
decided what your next task will be?

10
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:08,720
You haven't done much about the repair shop
while I was away, so I'll start with that.

11
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:12,200
I had an idea to put it on the edge of
the estate with access to the village.

12
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:13,790
For passing trade?

13
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:14,910
Why not?

14
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:16,900
Is Papa ready for that?

15
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:19,680
Hmm. You must be gentle with him.

16
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:21,550
When does Mr Mason move in?

17
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:22,870
His equipment's already there.

18
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:25,750
And the Drewes leave on Wednesday,
so there's no point in waiting.

19
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:27,510
And what about his old farm?

20
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:29,710
Mr Henderson's taken over the land.

21
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:32,710
He's going to move his uncle into
the house, much good may it do him.

22
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:34,750
Nay. There's no need for bitterness now.

23
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:36,270
Things have worked out well.

24
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:38,310
- You're right.
- Oh, I tell you what.

25
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:42,240
Why don't we go over on the day? We'll
take a picnic tea and lend him a hand.

26
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:44,550
I could come, too. Another pair of hands.

27
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:46,940
And very welcome, I have no doubt.

28
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:50,720
Good news?

29
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:53,470
Oh, it's just a friend who's
going to be in London.

30
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:55,470
- He wants to meet up.
- He?

31
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:56,790
It's nothing like that.

32
00:01:56,840 --> 00:01:59,750
It's Bertie Pelham, the
agent from Brancaster Castle.

33
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:01,910
I remember him. He shot with us.

34
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:05,440
And he helped get the magazine
out during my night of terror.

35
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:07,430
The Dowager Countess.

36
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:09,310
Hello, Granny. What are you doing here?

37
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:12,840
My dear. I am in need of a favour.

38
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:15,270
Is Cora about?

39
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:17,870
Er, no, she's got some
charity thing in the village.

40
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:19,260
Oh, good.

41
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:20,790
Why don't I like the sound of that?

42
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:22,440
I'll leave you.

43
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:26,160
The Minister of Health
is paying us a visit.

44
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:28,110
Mr Chamberlain? I don't think so.

45
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:32,440
Now, listen. He's on an
inspection tour of the north.

46
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:34,870
He wants to see what's been
happening since the war.

47
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:37,920
- Seems very sensible.
- And I want him to come here.

48
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,640
I want him to listen to our arguments
against the York Hospital's plans.

49
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:44,270
Mama, what is the point?

50
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:46,070
Don't be jejune. You know very well,

51
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:50,160
one word from Westminster and
the scheme would be abandoned.

52
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:53,960
But why would he say the word, and
why would he ever come to Downton?

53
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:58,760
You know, Neville Chamberlain's
wife was born Anne de Vere Cole.

54
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:02,270
Guess who was her godfather.

55
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:03,790
You guess for me.

56
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:07,840
Your late papa, the
sixth Earl of Grantham.

57
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:11,600
He and her father served in the
Crimea together when they were young.

58
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:13,630
I have known her since she was born.

59
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:16,550
I admit I am quite interested, but
when it comes to getting him here,

60
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:20,280
I would say you have no more chance
than a cat in hell without claws.

61
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,400
We'll see.

62
00:03:26,320 --> 00:03:29,760
Mr Carson has suggested we might
have dinner in our cottage tonight,

63
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:31,710
so I thought I'd ask your advice.

64
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:33,910
Oh, is this going to be a regular thing?

65
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:36,190
I don't think so. Once in a while.

66
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:38,430
Well, there's pate left
over and some chops.

67
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:40,150
- I'll make up a basket.
- Would you?

68
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:41,630
Oh, that would be so kind.

69
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:43,150
When did you last cook?

70
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:44,430
I've done the odd thing...

71
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:47,840
Oh, well, of course, as we
all know, anyone can cook.

72
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:50,600
Don't be like that. It's not my idea.

73
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:51,790
But it might be nice.

74
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:53,230
Mrs Patmore's just jealous.

75
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:55,240
I'm sure she's not.

76
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:57,860
But I would be grateful for the basket.

77
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:01,030
Well, maybe I am.

78
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:04,550
I nearly forgot. That chap, Henry...

79
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:05,630
- Talbot?
- Exactly.

80
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:07,310
Yes, he telephoned while you were out.

81
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:08,870
He's going to be in Yorkshire on Wednesday

82
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:11,830
to look at a car and he wants
you to watch him doing it.

83
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:14,440
Hmm. To watch him looking at a car?

84
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:17,520
Well, not quite. He's driving
it round some track nearby.

85
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:18,870
- At Catterick.
- Testing it.

86
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:20,270
Seeing how it handles.

87
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:21,950
- I could take you, if you like.
- Would you?

88
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:23,550
Well, I'd like to see it.

89
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:25,950
- How about you?
- I'll be in London on Wednesday.

90
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,030
Edith has a date.

91
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:28,950
- No, I don't.
- Of course not.

92
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:30,310
What do you mean, "Of course not"?

93
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:32,510
Doctor Clarkson seems to be coming round.

94
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:35,150
Isobel's bringing him for a
drink tonight with Dickie Merton.

95
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:37,150
So that's why Mama paid her visit.

96
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:38,510
When? What did she want?

97
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:40,870
Neville Chamberlain is coming north.

98
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:44,160
She wants him to dine here, so she
can convince him to support her.

99
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:47,070
If he's on a tour, it'll be far
too late to alter his schedule.

100
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:50,600
That's what I said, but she seems
to think she can persuade him.

101
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:53,080
Is it wrong to hope she's mistaken?

102
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:58,550
Anything interesting?

103
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:03,760
It's Sergeant Willis saying he'll collect
me on Wednesday at nine, to go to York.

104
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:05,380
For the trial.

105
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:07,870
Would you like me to come, too?

106
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:09,950
- Could you get the time off?
- Well, I could ask.

107
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:11,910
You shouldn't have to
go to the trial alone.

108
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:12,990
What trial?

109
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:15,550
Miss Baxter has been called
to be a witness on Wednesday.

110
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:17,870
I want to go along, keep her company.

111
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:20,590
- I think you should.
- Mr Carson will understand.

112
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:21,870
I'm only a character witness.

113
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:24,150
Character witnesses can
make all the difference.

114
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:25,750
Why do they want you to testify?

115
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:28,510
There's a man being
tried for theft and I...

116
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:29,830
I knew him once.

117
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:32,030
Will your words affect the outcome?

118
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:34,230
- Possibly.
- You must think about it carefully.

119
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:37,380
She has thought about it,
Mr Bates. Very carefully.

120
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:39,590
- Where were you?
- Oh, I'm sorry, Mr Barrow.

121
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:42,270
It was only coffee for the family,
so I thought Andy could manage.

122
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:44,990
He could. But it's for me to say, not you.

123
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,030
I'm going into the village in
a while. Do you want to come?

124
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:52,590
Thanks, but no, thanks.

125
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:54,030
I thought you needed some things.

126
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:55,310
Nothing urgent.

127
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:56,630
Right. Is that it?

128
00:05:56,680 --> 00:05:58,080
Er... Yes.

129
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:02,510
I'm glad to see you here again, Dickie.

130
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:05,070
Well, it's very good to be here, but
it's only about the row, I'm afraid.

131
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:06,150
Oh, never mind that.

132
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:08,710
We've brought Doctor Clarkson with
us because there's something that you,

133
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:10,910
or at any rate, Cora, will want to hear.

134
00:06:10,960 --> 00:06:13,190
Do I detect that you're changing sides?

135
00:06:13,240 --> 00:06:14,640
Er, maybe.

136
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:15,990
Have you told my mother?

137
00:06:16,040 --> 00:06:18,910
Poor man. If he's changing his
mind, don't let's put him off.

138
00:06:18,960 --> 00:06:20,750
Oh, that reminds me. I
have a message from her.

139
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:24,760
She says that the Health Minister
is happy to dine here this Friday.

140
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:26,790
What? How did she manage that?

141
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:28,550
She must have found a
way to blackmail him.

142
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:30,750
- You don't mean that.
- I wouldn't put it past her.

143
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:34,280
If she's convinced him to come here,
she can make him condemn the scheme.

144
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:36,070
Well, she mustn't.

145
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:38,470
And I want the three of
you here to support me.

146
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:40,260
Aye, aye, Captain.

147
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:48,190
Is Mr Barrow getting on your nerves?

148
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:51,720
- Only earlier, you seemed to --
- No, it's not that.

149
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:53,110
He only means to be friendly.

150
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:55,750
I know. And he was good to
me when I first arrived.

151
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:57,670
So, what is it, then?

152
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:00,000
Well...

153
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:03,070
Since I've come, I've got to
know a bit more about him.

154
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:05,720
I don't like to say with a lady present.

155
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,190
We both know Mr Barrow pretty well.

156
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:10,510
The point is, I wouldn't want
to give him any wrong ideas.

157
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:11,790
I'm not sure that's fair.

158
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:13,910
Fair or not, I think it's
better if he knows what's what.

159
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:15,870
I bear him no ill will, mind.

160
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:17,070
We are what we are.

161
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:18,990
Isn't that the truth?

162
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:23,990
My compliments to the chef.

163
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:26,240
Who is Mrs Patmore, not me.

164
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:27,830
What was Mr Molesley asking?

165
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:31,760
Er, he wants to accompany Miss
Baxter into York on Wednesday morning.

166
00:07:32,600 --> 00:07:34,310
Apparently, you've said she can go.

167
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:35,830
I have, yes.

168
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:38,070
- Will you let him?
- I don't see why not.

169
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:40,710
- What's it for?
- Sergeant Willis needs her help.

170
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:42,590
Sergeant Willis again?

171
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:46,120
Do other butlers have to contend with
the police arriving every ten minutes?

172
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:47,270
Not often.

173
00:07:47,320 --> 00:07:49,120
Now, are you ready?

174
00:07:50,200 --> 00:07:51,340
Hmm.

175
00:07:54,200 --> 00:07:56,310
Are... Are these done enough?

176
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:57,600
Yes.

177
00:07:57,680 --> 00:07:59,470
Shall I fetch the vegetables?

178
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:02,360
This plate's cold, which is a pity.

179
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:09,680
Ah, what's this?

180
00:08:09,760 --> 00:08:10,910
What do you think?

181
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:12,630
Bubble and squeak.

182
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:14,840
As a vegetable with lamb?

183
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:16,720
I like it with lamb.

184
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:20,520
Well, we mustn't let it get cold.

185
00:08:25,480 --> 00:08:27,950
This knife could do with sharpening.

186
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:37,830
All settled in for the night?

187
00:08:37,880 --> 00:08:39,910
She is, but she won't sleep a wink.

188
00:08:39,960 --> 00:08:42,110
She's too annoyed and I don't blame her.

189
00:08:42,160 --> 00:08:45,150
- What's happened now?
- She says Doctor Clarkson has only planned

190
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:47,470
to throw her over for Mrs Crawley.

191
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:49,390
- She's seen it coming.
- What do you mean?

192
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:52,430
He doesn't want to support
her ladyship any more.

193
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:54,400
What cheek!

194
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:56,070
Don't go working yourself up.

195
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:58,960
Who does he think he is?

196
00:08:59,040 --> 00:09:01,030
Jumped up little saw-bones!

197
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:02,950
I should steer clear.

198
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:05,120
No doubt.

199
00:09:05,200 --> 00:09:07,830
But you and I don't think
alike, do we, Mr Spratt?

200
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:10,020
On this or any other subject.

201
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
I'll wish you good night.

202
00:09:19,480 --> 00:09:20,750
When's your train?

203
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:23,230
- Nine. - Is this
another magazine crisis?

204
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:25,390
No. But I am interviewing
possible editors.

205
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:26,550
Women?

206
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:28,910
- Women. - Well, I approve.
Good luck with that.

207
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:31,430
- But then, who's the date with?
- It's not a date.

208
00:09:31,480 --> 00:09:33,950
It's just a friend, as I keep saying.

209
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:37,280
What are you up to today? Haven't
you got some plan with a racing car?

210
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:39,870
Later. We're looking for a site
for the repair shop this morning.

211
00:09:39,920 --> 00:09:41,230
Then we'll go over to
Catterick after lunch.

212
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:43,750
I want to look in on Mason before we do.

213
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:46,310
- He's moving in today.
- As long as the pigs are settled.

214
00:09:46,360 --> 00:09:48,070
You joke, but I am concerned.

215
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:50,990
I've asked him to take over, but
pig-keeping needs physical strength,

216
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:53,150
- now I think of it.
- You're right, of course.

217
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:54,550
They're strong and can be dangerous.

218
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:55,710
Ask him if he's up to it.

219
00:09:55,760 --> 00:09:58,190
- I think I must.
- A day of racing cars and pigs.

220
00:09:58,240 --> 00:10:00,110
Who could better that?

221
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:03,640
- Who's dining at home this time?
- Nobody.

222
00:10:03,720 --> 00:10:05,390
It's to welcome Daisy's Mr Mason.

223
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:06,790
He's moving in today.

224
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:08,710
Wish him well from me.

225
00:10:08,760 --> 00:10:12,240
And I hope he gets more fun out
of his hamper than Mr Carson did.

226
00:10:12,320 --> 00:10:13,430
Why? What was the matter with it?

227
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:15,430
Nothing was the matter with it.

228
00:10:15,480 --> 00:10:19,400
Except for the fact that, I don't
seem to cook like his mother.

229
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:24,080
Oh, I think the correct response
is to say "Men!" and sigh.

230
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:25,880
What's this?

231
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:27,880
Don't mind us.

232
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:30,630
- How can I help?
- Miss Baxter is expecting me?

233
00:10:30,680 --> 00:10:32,680
Daisy, run and fetch her.

234
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,030
Look after Miss Baxter, Sergeant.

235
00:10:35,080 --> 00:10:38,080
- She's in a very frail frame of mind.
- I'll do me best.

236
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:41,360
But we're after catching a nasty fish
and he's bound to thresh about a bit.

237
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:43,110
And we'll get caught in the spray?

238
00:10:43,160 --> 00:10:44,830
Good luck, love.

239
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:54,760
If we put the gate here, you'd be on
a lane that links with the main road.

240
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:57,510
You'd get power and plumbing
from the supply at King's Barrow.

241
00:10:57,560 --> 00:10:59,750
Why don't we put the repair shed there?

242
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:01,350
King's Barrow is a working farm.

243
00:11:01,400 --> 00:11:04,230
There isn't a spare farmyard
half as accessible as here.

244
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:06,480
We ought to look at the costs.

245
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:08,870
It's good of you to come with me today.

246
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:11,190
Glad to, but he won't want me there.

247
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:15,240
Nonsense. You have far more
in common with him than I do.

248
00:11:15,320 --> 00:11:16,920
Is it serious?

249
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,080
He's attractive and nice,

250
00:11:19,160 --> 00:11:22,440
and it's good to remember
I'm a youngish woman again.

251
00:11:22,520 --> 00:11:23,670
But that's all.

252
00:11:23,720 --> 00:11:25,060
Youngish?

253
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:30,000
I don't mean to sound snobbish,
but I won't marry down.

254
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:33,430
Was Mr Matthew Crawley so
very special in that way?

255
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:36,800
Matthew was the heir to
the earldom and estate.

256
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:38,990
I don't want to be
grander than my husband.

257
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:40,390
Or richer.

258
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:44,160
It may surprise you, but I agree
it's important to be balanced,

259
00:11:44,240 --> 00:11:46,790
that one should not be far
stronger than the other.

260
00:11:46,840 --> 00:11:50,200
I just don't think it has much
to do with money or position.

261
00:11:50,280 --> 00:11:52,800
Is that how you felt about Sybil?

262
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:55,510
To all of you, she had
everything and I had nothing.

263
00:11:55,560 --> 00:11:59,200
She was the great lady, and
I the man who drove the cars,

264
00:11:59,280 --> 00:12:01,520
but that wasn't true for us.

265
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:04,760
We were evenly matched, Sybil and I.

266
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:07,440
She was strong in her beliefs, so was I.

267
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:11,240
We were a marriage of
equals. We were very happy.

268
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:13,950
I think we see that now.

269
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,710
The family, I mean.

270
00:12:15,760 --> 00:12:17,510
Not at first, you're right.

271
00:12:17,560 --> 00:12:18,900
But now.

272
00:12:30,600 --> 00:12:32,870
- Good day to you.
- Good day?

273
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:35,350
A wonder you've got the
nerve to speak to me.

274
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:38,360
- I beg your pardon?
- Throwing over my lady,

275
00:12:38,440 --> 00:12:40,910
when she's been running this
village since you were eating

276
00:12:40,960 --> 00:12:42,710
porridge in the glen with your mummy.

277
00:12:42,760 --> 00:12:45,430
I don't believe I am required
to justify my actions to you.

278
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:47,030
Because you can't.

279
00:12:47,080 --> 00:12:50,560
Tell me, what would you
call it? Gratitude?

280
00:12:51,280 --> 00:12:53,150
Because I'd call it treason.

281
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:58,360
Would you? Well, I call it impertinence,
to a degree that is not acceptable.

282
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:01,800
And I'm afraid you haven't
heard the last of this.

283
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:06,230
Well, you look very chirpy.

284
00:13:06,280 --> 00:13:07,590
Oh, I think we'll have fun.

285
00:13:07,640 --> 00:13:09,590
Besides, I've had some news.

286
00:13:09,640 --> 00:13:11,590
They've nearly finished
the work on my house.

287
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:13,230
- Oh, that was quick.
- Was it?

288
00:13:13,280 --> 00:13:16,110
It doesn't feel very
quick, but it's almost done.

289
00:13:16,160 --> 00:13:18,110
Now you just have to find some customers.

290
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:20,480
But it's exciting, isn't it?

291
00:13:27,920 --> 00:13:30,310
- Did you hear?
- That he changed his plea? Yes.

292
00:13:30,360 --> 00:13:32,230
I was in court. I've only just come out.

293
00:13:32,280 --> 00:13:35,760
I expect when he heard that you turned
up, he must've known it was pointless.

294
00:13:35,840 --> 00:13:37,640
So I've been spared.

295
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:40,310
How do you feel?

296
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:42,670
In one way, I feel relieved, of course.

297
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:45,760
The newspapers won't find me and
there'll be no repercussions.

298
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:48,190
- But?
- I suppose I'd worked myself up

299
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:51,280
into facing him across courtroom,
this man who ruined my life,

300
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:55,200
and now it feels a bit anticlimactic.

301
00:13:57,880 --> 00:14:01,280
Shall I go back in and ask him
to plead not guilty after all?

302
00:14:02,200 --> 00:14:04,400
No, thank you.

303
00:14:05,440 --> 00:14:08,640
We should leave. Sergeant
Willis is fetching the car.

304
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:20,320
It does me good to see a friendly
woman bustling about a kitchen.

305
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:23,520
I've got tea for all of us,
and a snack for you later on.

306
00:14:23,600 --> 00:14:24,830
You're an angel of mercy.

307
00:14:24,880 --> 00:14:25,990
Are we interrupting?

308
00:14:26,040 --> 00:14:27,110
Not a bit of it, m'lady.

309
00:14:27,160 --> 00:14:28,390
You're very welcome here.

310
00:14:28,440 --> 00:14:31,150
We had just wanted to look
in to see how you were doing.

311
00:14:31,200 --> 00:14:33,430
Daisy can tell you where
to find our office.

312
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:35,820
Daisy will be a great help to me.

313
00:14:36,360 --> 00:14:38,640
We wanted to discuss the pigs.

314
00:14:38,720 --> 00:14:40,790
- Shall we go outside?
- We can talk here, m'lady.

315
00:14:40,840 --> 00:14:42,750
There's nothing private about it.

316
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:44,030
Very well.

317
00:14:44,080 --> 00:14:46,670
Of course, I understand you
have a lot of experience.

318
00:14:46,720 --> 00:14:47,870
A great deal.

319
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:49,470
I'm top at pigs.

320
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:52,030
But Lady Mary is a little worried
about the physical side of it.

321
00:14:52,080 --> 00:14:55,120
Prising a boar off a sow or taking
the piglets from their mother.

322
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:57,920
Is this because I'm older than I was?

323
00:14:59,120 --> 00:15:02,920
Of course, you may have already
chosen a farm hand to help you.

324
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:06,960
We've discussed it, Mr Mason and I.

325
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:09,390
He'll give me warning when there's
any chance of a bit of strong arm

326
00:15:09,440 --> 00:15:10,870
and I'll walk down from the house.

327
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:13,590
So you'll be there for the servicing,
and the separating and the rest of it?

328
00:15:13,640 --> 00:15:16,270
We'll plan it round when
Mr Carson can release me.

329
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:18,670
- It's not every day.
- That's very good of you, Andrew.

330
00:15:18,720 --> 00:15:20,830
I want to train in the
care of pigs, m'lady.

331
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:22,790
I want to learn as much
as I can about farming.

332
00:15:22,840 --> 00:15:25,960
Oh, I see. Well, good.

333
00:15:26,040 --> 00:15:27,710
That seems to settle it.

334
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:31,260
We must go. We're on our way
to Catterick and we're late.

335
00:15:31,640 --> 00:15:34,640
- We hope you'll be very happy here.
- Thank you.

336
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:43,760
Well, I do want to train.

337
00:15:43,840 --> 00:15:45,310
I do want to learn.

338
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:47,360
It were no word of a lie.

339
00:15:48,640 --> 00:15:51,390
Why does Lord Hexham spend
so much time in Tangier?

340
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:53,070
I suppose he likes it there.

341
00:15:53,120 --> 00:15:55,910
If I had Brancaster Castle, I'm
not sure I'd ever want to leave.

342
00:15:55,960 --> 00:15:57,360
I agree.

343
00:15:57,440 --> 00:15:58,990
He's not really a country type.

344
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:01,390
More arty than sporty,
if you know what I mean.

345
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:03,430
He doesn't hunt or shoot?

346
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:05,480
Hardly. He paints.

347
00:16:05,560 --> 00:16:06,990
What does he paint?

348
00:16:07,040 --> 00:16:09,680
The young men of Tangier, mainly.

349
00:16:09,760 --> 00:16:11,950
You know, scenes of local life.

350
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,920
And he's never wanted to marry?

351
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:16,470
I wouldn't quite say that.

352
00:16:16,520 --> 00:16:18,310
It's always been sort of
understood that he and his cousin,

353
00:16:18,360 --> 00:16:20,350
Adela Graham, will marry eventually.

354
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:21,830
Understood by whom?

355
00:16:21,880 --> 00:16:23,950
By the two sets of parents.

356
00:16:24,560 --> 00:16:26,100
How romantic.

357
00:16:27,120 --> 00:16:28,670
But you like him?

358
00:16:28,720 --> 00:16:31,150
You wouldn't ever want to
find something else to do?

359
00:16:31,200 --> 00:16:33,110
I'd hate to leave Brancaster

360
00:16:33,160 --> 00:16:36,360
and I'm very fond of Cousin Peter,
even if he isn't a countryman.

361
00:16:36,440 --> 00:16:38,840
He hasn't a nasty bone in his body.

362
00:16:39,640 --> 00:16:41,550
What are we doing this evening?

363
00:16:41,600 --> 00:16:43,030
Have I got your evening, too?

364
00:16:43,080 --> 00:16:45,710
Come to my flat for a
drink, I'll show it to you.

365
00:16:45,760 --> 00:16:47,390
Then we can go somewhere else. You choose.

366
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:49,110
What a racy plan.

367
00:16:49,200 --> 00:16:51,670
Not as racy as all that, worse luck.

368
00:16:52,080 --> 00:16:53,710
But I'd like your opinion.

369
00:16:53,760 --> 00:16:55,630
And you shall have it.

370
00:17:17,240 --> 00:17:19,870
Charlie's going to beat him
again. It infuriates him.

371
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:21,510
Mr Rogers is a good driver.

372
00:17:21,560 --> 00:17:23,630
I know, but they take such risks.

373
00:17:23,680 --> 00:17:25,510
I hate it. I just hate it.

374
00:17:25,560 --> 00:17:27,390
There's no such thing
as slow motor racing.

375
00:17:27,440 --> 00:17:28,920
Even so.

376
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,150
And there's no such thing as safe love.

377
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:33,430
Real love means giving
someone the power to hurt you.

378
00:17:33,480 --> 00:17:35,620
Which I won't concede easily.

379
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:04,830
She went well.

380
00:18:04,880 --> 00:18:07,750
She's perfect, Charles.
Don't change a thing.

381
00:18:08,880 --> 00:18:10,590
I think I started after you.

382
00:18:10,640 --> 00:18:12,310
You're a bad loser!

383
00:18:12,360 --> 00:18:14,110
- Well?
- How fast.

384
00:18:14,160 --> 00:18:15,990
That is the general idea.

385
00:18:16,040 --> 00:18:17,670
It looks like she handles well.

386
00:18:17,720 --> 00:18:18,870
Will you take her to Brooklands?

387
00:18:18,920 --> 00:18:20,070
Brooklands and other tracks.

388
00:18:20,120 --> 00:18:22,110
I think I've found my new
car. What do you say, Charlie?

389
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:24,270
Well, she must be good to beat me.

390
00:18:24,320 --> 00:18:26,070
- If you did.
- Oh, I did and I will again.

391
00:18:26,120 --> 00:18:27,550
Then we must celebrate.

392
00:18:27,600 --> 00:18:29,430
Well, there's a pub at Catterick.

393
00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:31,670
Not for me, I'm afraid. I have to go home.

394
00:18:31,720 --> 00:18:33,190
- Oh.
- Let's go anyway.

395
00:18:33,240 --> 00:18:35,440
Okay. Just let me get changed.

396
00:18:36,720 --> 00:18:38,390
Help him to enjoy it.

397
00:18:38,440 --> 00:18:42,440
You don't have to marry him, but you
do have to let him enjoy this moment.

398
00:18:43,880 --> 00:18:45,910
You're not nervous? Because I would be.

399
00:18:45,960 --> 00:18:47,990
No. I'm looking forward to it.

400
00:18:48,040 --> 00:18:49,430
We'll make a pig man of you yet.

401
00:18:49,480 --> 00:18:51,510
I'll lend you some books
when we go inside.

402
00:18:51,560 --> 00:18:53,150
- Books?
- On pig breeding and care.

403
00:18:53,200 --> 00:18:54,950
You need to know the theory of it.

404
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:56,390
You'll be glad of the knowledge.

405
00:18:56,440 --> 00:18:58,710
It makes the work more logical.

406
00:18:58,800 --> 00:19:00,400
Well, then.

407
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:03,940
Thank you, we'll be in touch.

408
00:19:05,680 --> 00:19:07,590
- How many more?
- Just one.

409
00:19:07,640 --> 00:19:08,950
Miss Edmunds.

410
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:10,310
Send her in.

411
00:19:10,360 --> 00:19:12,230
If you'd like to go in now.

412
00:19:12,280 --> 00:19:13,950
Please sit down.

413
00:19:14,680 --> 00:19:17,590
I've been reading about
your many achievements.

414
00:19:17,640 --> 00:19:19,270
Oh, I see you were born in '92.

415
00:19:19,320 --> 00:19:22,800
I know I might seem too young to be
an editor, but I do have experience.

416
00:19:22,880 --> 00:19:25,110
I was just going to say
we're born in the same year.

417
00:19:25,160 --> 00:19:28,120
Er, have you seen a copy
of our latest edition?

418
00:19:29,560 --> 00:19:30,910
I produced it, actually.

419
00:19:30,960 --> 00:19:34,560
Our editor had left, so it was down
to me and the rest of the office,

420
00:19:34,640 --> 00:19:37,350
and during one horror-filled
night, we did it.

421
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:38,950
But then are you sure you need me?

422
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:42,720
Oh, I am. But I have at least proved
you're old enough to be an editor.

423
00:19:42,800 --> 00:19:44,480
Because I was.

424
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:47,080
1892 seems a million years ago now.

425
00:19:47,160 --> 00:19:49,560
Another time, another age.

426
00:19:49,640 --> 00:19:51,310
That might be worth exploring.

427
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:53,670
Victorian babies grown into modern women.

428
00:19:53,720 --> 00:19:55,790
And the price they've paid.

429
00:19:56,200 --> 00:19:58,070
Oh. Thank you, Audrey.

430
00:19:59,640 --> 00:20:01,830
Mmm, this is something like it.

431
00:20:01,880 --> 00:20:04,360
I think you'll do well here.

432
00:20:04,440 --> 00:20:05,670
So do I.

433
00:20:05,720 --> 00:20:08,590
And you know what I wish?
That our Daisy would live here.

434
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:12,000
You can still work at the big
house, but make a home with me.

435
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:13,590
- I don't know.
- What is it?

436
00:20:13,640 --> 00:20:15,390
A 20-minute walk? What's that?

437
00:20:15,440 --> 00:20:16,710
I'll have to think about it.

438
00:20:16,760 --> 00:20:19,870
I wouldn't have to think long.
This place is like heaven to me.

439
00:20:19,920 --> 00:20:22,960
You never set foot off a
pavement for 18 years,

440
00:20:23,040 --> 00:20:25,470
and now it's all harvests and pig farming.

441
00:20:25,520 --> 00:20:27,910
Well, not everyone's right
for what they're born to.

442
00:20:27,960 --> 00:20:31,400
Very true. These are the books I spoke of.

443
00:20:31,480 --> 00:20:33,830
You'll learn most from the work, I know,

444
00:20:33,880 --> 00:20:36,030
but, er, they'll give you a grounding.

445
00:20:36,080 --> 00:20:37,710
- If you're serious.
- I am.

446
00:20:37,760 --> 00:20:42,080
Now, what else can I give you?
Another cup of tea, why not?

447
00:20:42,160 --> 00:20:43,900
Don't mind if I do.

448
00:20:47,920 --> 00:20:51,240
I'm afraid Mummy, and Daddy,
and Aunt Edith are all away,

449
00:20:51,320 --> 00:20:53,710
so you must make do with Granny and Donk.

450
00:20:53,760 --> 00:20:55,960
Is Granny Violet a Red Indian?

451
00:20:56,040 --> 00:20:57,550
Why on earth do you say that?

452
00:20:57,600 --> 00:21:00,280
Nanny said she was on the war path.

453
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:05,360
Come and look at these
books I've put out for you.

454
00:21:05,440 --> 00:21:08,920
These are some of the places
Donk and I have visited.

455
00:21:10,400 --> 00:21:14,640
Chamberlain's office rang to confirm
that he is dining here on Friday.

456
00:21:15,080 --> 00:21:16,230
I wish he weren't.

457
00:21:16,280 --> 00:21:17,950
But you'll line up the opposition?

458
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:19,590
Certainly. I already have.

459
00:21:19,640 --> 00:21:21,230
What a nuisance it is.

460
00:21:21,280 --> 00:21:22,630
Mama is not a good loser.

461
00:21:22,680 --> 00:21:24,350
She's had so little experience.

462
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:27,760
You couldn't just back off and
let the cards fall as they may.

463
00:21:27,840 --> 00:21:31,320
Robert, for 30 years, I've striven
to let your mother have her own way

464
00:21:31,400 --> 00:21:32,950
but this is too important.

465
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:34,910
I appreciate that her
motives are honourable,

466
00:21:34,960 --> 00:21:38,120
but that's not enough when
she's damaging people's lives.

467
00:21:38,640 --> 00:21:39,670
Oh.

468
00:21:39,720 --> 00:21:42,190
We can cancel, if I'm
allowed to say you're ill.

469
00:21:42,240 --> 00:21:44,510
I'm not ill enough, and
she'd only rearrange it.

470
00:21:44,560 --> 00:21:46,910
She can obviously get Chamberlain
to do whatever she wants,

471
00:21:46,960 --> 00:21:48,630
though heaven knows why.

472
00:21:48,680 --> 00:21:49,910
What's this?

473
00:21:49,960 --> 00:21:52,190
That's the Sphinx, darling. In Egypt.

474
00:21:52,240 --> 00:21:54,680
- What?
- The Sphinx.

475
00:21:54,760 --> 00:21:57,840
A creature of secrets
that she never reveals.

476
00:21:57,920 --> 00:22:00,000
Rather like Granny Violet.

477
00:22:02,600 --> 00:22:05,320
What time will you get there, on Friday?

478
00:22:05,400 --> 00:22:07,640
Well, not much before eight.

479
00:22:07,720 --> 00:22:12,040
I want to be in possession of the room
by the time Mr Chamberlain arrives.

480
00:22:13,320 --> 00:22:15,200
Oh, dear!

481
00:22:15,280 --> 00:22:16,590
Bad news?

482
00:22:16,640 --> 00:22:17,790
Oh, dear...

483
00:22:17,840 --> 00:22:19,750
I wouldn't have brought it if I'd known.

484
00:22:19,800 --> 00:22:22,750
Er, Spratt, would you ask
Denker to come up here please?

485
00:22:22,800 --> 00:22:24,540
Of course, m'lady.

486
00:22:24,800 --> 00:22:26,190
You're quite agitated.

487
00:22:26,240 --> 00:22:27,910
Denker has disgraced herself.

488
00:22:27,960 --> 00:22:29,550
Well, how distressing for you.

489
00:22:29,600 --> 00:22:32,160
It's not that so much.

490
00:22:32,240 --> 00:22:34,870
It means I shall have to find a new maid.

491
00:22:34,920 --> 00:22:37,120
Yes, I see. A real punishment.

492
00:22:38,320 --> 00:22:39,350
M'lady?

493
00:22:39,400 --> 00:22:41,670
Is it true you called
Doctor Clarkson a traitor?

494
00:22:41,720 --> 00:22:44,800
What? Surely not? Denker?

495
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:48,840
I just thought he'd behaved
very badly towards your ladyship.

496
00:22:48,920 --> 00:22:52,040
It is not your place even to
have opinions of my acquaintance.

497
00:22:52,120 --> 00:22:53,710
Let alone express them.

498
00:22:53,760 --> 00:22:58,120
He can't claim your friendship now?
Not when he's turned against you!

499
00:22:58,200 --> 00:23:02,240
If I withdrew my friendship from
everyone who had spoken ill of me,

500
00:23:02,320 --> 00:23:04,030
my address book would be empty.

501
00:23:04,080 --> 00:23:05,150
Yes, but surely...

502
00:23:05,200 --> 00:23:09,400
For a lady's maid to insult a
physician in the open street!

503
00:23:09,480 --> 00:23:12,800
You've read too many novels, Denker.
You've seen too many moving pictures.

504
00:23:12,880 --> 00:23:14,670
I was sticking up for you.

505
00:23:14,720 --> 00:23:17,590
And for that, I will
write a tepid character,

506
00:23:17,640 --> 00:23:20,230
which may enable you to
find employment elsewhere.

507
00:23:20,280 --> 00:23:22,510
But from this house,
you must go, forthwith.

508
00:23:22,560 --> 00:23:23,590
But m'lady...

509
00:23:23,640 --> 00:23:26,190
You may stay tonight, but
you must go tomorrow.

510
00:23:26,240 --> 00:23:27,380
Go!

511
00:23:29,720 --> 00:23:34,000
Are you sure? I can't believe Doctor
Clarkson could wish her to lose her position.

512
00:23:34,080 --> 00:23:36,150
Then he shouldn't have sent it.

513
00:23:36,200 --> 00:23:40,080
When we unleash the dogs of war,
we must go where they take us.

514
00:23:49,260 --> 00:23:51,770
Sorry about that. There
was rather a queue.

515
00:23:51,820 --> 00:23:53,130
It's popular. That's a good sign.

516
00:23:53,180 --> 00:23:54,570
Oh, you don't know the place?

517
00:23:54,620 --> 00:23:57,330
You'll laugh at me, but I've
hardly ever been in a public house.

518
00:23:57,380 --> 00:24:00,620
Matthew wasn't really a pub man and
Papa goes into the Grantham Arms

519
00:24:00,700 --> 00:24:02,890
about once a year to have
a drink with the tenants.

520
00:24:02,940 --> 00:24:05,890
Well, I'm afraid my life is
an altogether rougher affair.

521
00:24:05,940 --> 00:24:07,680
Consider me warned.

522
00:24:08,900 --> 00:24:10,730
So the car's a success?

523
00:24:10,780 --> 00:24:13,250
Well, I wasn't convinced
it would be, but it is.

524
00:24:13,300 --> 00:24:14,570
You must have a go sometime.

525
00:24:14,620 --> 00:24:15,690
I hope that's a real offer.

526
00:24:15,740 --> 00:24:17,970
You know, I didn't realise
you were so keen, Tom. Blast!

527
00:24:18,020 --> 00:24:19,290
You could have driven her today.

528
00:24:19,340 --> 00:24:21,010
You know I came to Downton as a chauffeur?

529
00:24:21,060 --> 00:24:22,130
Oh, Mary told me.

530
00:24:22,180 --> 00:24:24,810
But then, not every chauffeur
has a real love of cars.

531
00:24:24,860 --> 00:24:26,010
That's true enough.

532
00:24:26,060 --> 00:24:27,730
Oh, I'll tell you who was
talking about you the other day.

533
00:24:27,780 --> 00:24:28,930
Evelyn Napier.

534
00:24:28,980 --> 00:24:30,930
- Oh, how is he?
- He's well.

535
00:24:30,980 --> 00:24:34,500
Still single, of course, and, I
suspect, still pining for you.

536
00:24:34,580 --> 00:24:37,580
He will pine in vain, but
I'm very fond of him.

537
00:24:37,660 --> 00:24:39,610
La Belle Dame sans Merci.

538
00:24:39,660 --> 00:24:41,250
What does that mean?

539
00:24:41,300 --> 00:24:44,020
It means Lady Mary knows what she's about.

540
00:24:45,260 --> 00:24:48,380
Tell you what, next time you're down
south, why don't we all have dinner?

541
00:24:48,460 --> 00:24:49,970
I'd love that.

542
00:24:50,020 --> 00:24:52,410
- You are funny.
- What do you mean?

543
00:24:52,460 --> 00:24:54,650
The way you have to keep making
reasons for why you'll meet.

544
00:24:54,700 --> 00:24:57,370
You to watch him drive cars, you
to have dinner with a friend.

545
00:24:57,420 --> 00:25:00,420
Why can't you just say, "I'd
love to spend more time with you,

546
00:25:00,500 --> 00:25:01,650
"when can we do it?"

547
00:25:01,700 --> 00:25:04,290
You see? He may have
assimilated in some ways,

548
00:25:04,340 --> 00:25:07,010
but he still fights playing by the rules.

549
00:25:15,380 --> 00:25:17,020
What are those?

550
00:25:17,100 --> 00:25:19,300
Oh! I didn't hear you come in.

551
00:25:20,020 --> 00:25:24,620
Mr Mason's lent me some books on
pig-rearing and farming, generally.

552
00:25:24,700 --> 00:25:26,010
I'm going to help with the pigs.

553
00:25:26,060 --> 00:25:27,860
Oh, I see.

554
00:25:27,940 --> 00:25:29,970
Which will you start with?

555
00:25:30,020 --> 00:25:31,690
The red one, I think.

556
00:25:31,740 --> 00:25:33,940
The "red one." Who's it by?

557
00:25:39,500 --> 00:25:41,260
FJ Connell.

558
00:25:41,340 --> 00:25:42,730
Does that mean anything to you?

559
00:25:42,780 --> 00:25:44,180
Not a lot.

560
00:25:54,420 --> 00:25:57,290
This must be the most sophisticated
room I've ever been in.

561
00:25:57,340 --> 00:25:58,970
The taste is all Michael's.

562
00:25:59,020 --> 00:26:01,490
He was very artistic and very clever.

563
00:26:01,540 --> 00:26:03,010
So, are you going to live here?

564
00:26:03,060 --> 00:26:04,930
I think I'll live here more.

565
00:26:04,980 --> 00:26:07,460
I'd like a life away from Downton.

566
00:26:07,540 --> 00:26:09,250
Because you like London?

567
00:26:09,300 --> 00:26:11,660
Well, the house, the estate,

568
00:26:11,740 --> 00:26:15,140
they're all Mary's now,
more than Papa realises.

569
00:26:15,220 --> 00:26:18,500
It's time for me to strike
out in my own direction,

570
00:26:18,580 --> 00:26:21,460
not just dawdle in Mary's wake.

571
00:26:21,540 --> 00:26:23,730
When do you go home this time?

572
00:26:23,780 --> 00:26:26,380
Tomorrow. My work is done here.

573
00:26:26,460 --> 00:26:29,170
I've found my new editor and
we've got a government minister

574
00:26:29,220 --> 00:26:31,930
coming for dinner on Friday,
so we're all on parade.

575
00:26:31,980 --> 00:26:33,570
That's very swanky.

576
00:26:33,620 --> 00:26:35,610
- Is your father political?
- Not at all.

577
00:26:35,660 --> 00:26:37,250
It's my grandmother who's invited him.

578
00:26:37,300 --> 00:26:38,410
How impressive!

579
00:26:38,460 --> 00:26:40,140
She is rather.

580
00:26:40,220 --> 00:26:41,690
Shall we go?

581
00:26:45,300 --> 00:26:47,420
I love the Cafe de Paris.

582
00:26:47,500 --> 00:26:48,890
How did you know to choose it?

583
00:26:48,940 --> 00:26:51,280
I knew we'd love the same things.

584
00:27:03,180 --> 00:27:05,500
God, what a relief.

585
00:27:05,580 --> 00:27:07,650
I thought I might be pushing my luck.

586
00:27:07,700 --> 00:27:09,940
Oh, no.

587
00:27:10,020 --> 00:27:12,210
I suppose you've guessed
how much I like you.

588
00:27:12,260 --> 00:27:13,570
You don't know me.

589
00:27:13,620 --> 00:27:17,100
I know you enough to think about
you all the time when we're apart.

590
00:27:17,180 --> 00:27:18,920
That's very sweet.

591
00:27:19,620 --> 00:27:22,260
Of course, I haven't much to offer.

592
00:27:22,340 --> 00:27:24,580
You have a great deal to offer

593
00:27:24,660 --> 00:27:27,700
and I am not sure I'm worthy of it,

594
00:27:27,780 --> 00:27:32,100
but for now let's just enjoy a good dinner

595
00:27:32,180 --> 00:27:33,650
and some dancing.

596
00:27:33,700 --> 00:27:35,440
No arguments here.

597
00:27:41,260 --> 00:27:43,940
- So it was a good day?
- Oh, yes.

598
00:27:44,020 --> 00:27:46,970
A happy ending to a trying
time for the poor man.

599
00:27:47,020 --> 00:27:48,650
Daisy fought well in his cause.

600
00:27:48,700 --> 00:27:50,570
If not always very sensibly.

601
00:27:50,620 --> 00:27:53,860
He's a lovely chap, though,
kind and considerate.

602
00:27:53,940 --> 00:27:56,890
Oh, he wants Daisy to live there,
but I suppose that's understandable.

603
00:27:56,940 --> 00:27:57,970
He must be lonely.

604
00:27:58,020 --> 00:28:00,490
He's not lonely. He's
lived on his own for years.

605
00:28:00,540 --> 00:28:03,130
- He's used to it.
- He enjoyed a bit of company today.

606
00:28:03,180 --> 00:28:04,570
He was just being polite.

607
00:28:04,620 --> 00:28:07,180
I expect he was longing for us to go.

608
00:28:11,740 --> 00:28:14,490
You mustn't mind when Mr Mason
makes new friends, Daisy,

609
00:28:14,540 --> 00:28:16,250
now that he's here among us.

610
00:28:16,300 --> 00:28:17,610
Of course I don't mind.

611
00:28:17,660 --> 00:28:19,410
I just think he's fine as he is.

612
00:28:19,460 --> 00:28:22,010
I'm ready to walk up to
the cottage if you are.

613
00:28:22,060 --> 00:28:24,850
- Of course. - Ah, Mrs
Patmore, I haven't thanked you

614
00:28:24,900 --> 00:28:26,010
for our supper the other night.

615
00:28:26,060 --> 00:28:27,210
I'm always happy to help.

616
00:28:27,260 --> 00:28:28,410
I'm very grateful.

617
00:28:28,460 --> 00:28:32,460
But another time, I wonder if you might go
through the cooking of it with Mrs Hughes.

618
00:28:32,540 --> 00:28:35,050
- Oh, yes? - It's been a
while since she's played

619
00:28:35,100 --> 00:28:38,580
with her patty pans, and she's
got some catching-up to do.

620
00:28:38,660 --> 00:28:40,330
You'd be glad of the help, wouldn't you?

621
00:28:40,380 --> 00:28:43,260
Very glad and very grateful.

622
00:28:43,740 --> 00:28:45,880
And now I must fetch my coat.

623
00:28:50,580 --> 00:28:52,130
I'm sorry, Miss Denker.

624
00:28:52,180 --> 00:28:54,770
I simply don't see I have a role to play.

625
00:28:54,820 --> 00:28:58,090
So you're just going to sit
there and let me be dismissed?

626
00:28:58,140 --> 00:28:59,690
How did it happen?

627
00:28:59,740 --> 00:29:02,740
- Were you drunk?
- No, I was not drunk!

628
00:29:02,820 --> 00:29:04,250
I was shocked!

629
00:29:04,300 --> 00:29:08,140
Shocked at the short memory of a
doctor who's failed his patroness!

630
00:29:08,220 --> 00:29:10,170
I doubt he sees it in that light.

631
00:29:10,220 --> 00:29:13,180
That's the way I saw it
and I acted accordingly.

632
00:29:13,260 --> 00:29:16,210
Am I to blame if I have
a very passionate nature?

633
00:29:16,260 --> 00:29:18,610
Any more of that talk and
I won't be able to sleep.

634
00:29:18,660 --> 00:29:23,140
You won't sleep a wink if I am
still sacked when we go upstairs.

635
00:29:23,220 --> 00:29:25,540
That's a risk I'll have to take.

636
00:29:31,060 --> 00:29:36,460
♪ Crying cockles and
mussels Alive, alive, oh ♪

637
00:29:36,860 --> 00:29:40,340
I'm dreading this dinner on Friday.

638
00:29:40,420 --> 00:29:42,330
So are we all, but we
have to get through it.

639
00:29:42,380 --> 00:29:47,660
I'm afraid Mama seems to see this argument as
the last battle, the last big fight of her life.

640
00:29:47,740 --> 00:29:50,780
If she loses, there'll be hell to pay.

641
00:29:50,860 --> 00:29:53,180
Then there'll be hell to pay.

642
00:29:53,260 --> 00:29:55,650
Added to which, I'm feeling pretty rough.

643
00:29:55,700 --> 00:29:58,780
I'm sure it's only indigestion,
but whether it is or not,

644
00:29:58,860 --> 00:30:00,970
I'll be glad of a chance
to put my feet up.

645
00:30:01,020 --> 00:30:03,730
It's too late to cancel, but I'd be
happy to manage the evening without you,

646
00:30:03,780 --> 00:30:04,810
if you're really ill.

647
00:30:04,860 --> 00:30:07,010
Mama would only say I'd ratted on her.

648
00:30:07,060 --> 00:30:08,810
No. As you say, I'll get through it

649
00:30:08,860 --> 00:30:11,660
and then take things
quietly for a few days.

650
00:30:22,820 --> 00:30:24,370
How did that happen?

651
00:30:24,420 --> 00:30:26,410
I threw a book and it caught it.

652
00:30:26,460 --> 00:30:29,460
Oh, yes. The red one.
Why did you throw it?

653
00:30:35,540 --> 00:30:37,880
Why did you throw the book, Andy?

654
00:30:41,780 --> 00:30:45,060
- You can't read, can you?
- No. I can't bloody read!

655
00:30:45,140 --> 00:30:47,050
Go on! Have a good laugh about it!

656
00:30:47,100 --> 00:30:48,410
I'm not laughing.

657
00:30:48,460 --> 00:30:50,610
You've been good at hiding
it. I must say that.

658
00:30:50,660 --> 00:30:52,650
Flicking through your magazines.

659
00:30:52,700 --> 00:30:54,840
I only look at the pictures.

660
00:30:57,020 --> 00:30:58,970
Why did you not learn at school?

661
00:30:59,020 --> 00:31:01,660
I fooled around until it was too late.

662
00:31:01,740 --> 00:31:04,740
I learned how to sign my name,
which was all I needed in service.

663
00:31:04,820 --> 00:31:06,810
But now you want to be a farmer.

664
00:31:06,860 --> 00:31:10,500
I could be a farm labourer,
but I want more than that.

665
00:31:10,580 --> 00:31:13,050
And if I can't read, then
it won't be possible.

666
00:31:13,100 --> 00:31:15,220
So another dream goes west.

667
00:31:15,300 --> 00:31:17,500
It doesn't have to.

668
00:31:17,580 --> 00:31:20,900
I'll teach you to read and
write, too, if you want.

669
00:31:20,980 --> 00:31:23,740
I must be too stupid.

670
00:31:23,820 --> 00:31:27,020
I've never picked it up so far, and
I would have if I had half a brain.

671
00:31:27,100 --> 00:31:30,060
That's not true. You're a clever lad.

672
00:31:30,140 --> 00:31:32,680
You will get the hang of it. Trust me.

673
00:31:33,740 --> 00:31:36,820
- But what would the others say?
- We won't tell them.

674
00:31:36,900 --> 00:31:39,770
We'll talk about it in
the morning, all right?

675
00:31:42,420 --> 00:31:43,900
Mr Barrow.

676
00:31:44,660 --> 00:31:47,340
I've not behaved well towards you.

677
00:31:48,220 --> 00:31:50,090
And I'm sorry for it.

678
00:31:50,900 --> 00:31:52,570
I've known worse.

679
00:31:53,940 --> 00:31:55,610
Good night, Andy.

680
00:32:03,820 --> 00:32:05,820
Oh! You're up early.

681
00:32:05,900 --> 00:32:08,170
I suppose you have quite a day ahead.

682
00:32:08,220 --> 00:32:09,970
- Are you packed?
- No.

683
00:32:10,020 --> 00:32:11,370
I have not packed.

684
00:32:11,420 --> 00:32:12,690
Shouldn't leave it too long.

685
00:32:12,740 --> 00:32:15,650
You should take up her
breakfast and dress her,

686
00:32:15,700 --> 00:32:19,180
to be sure of your reference,
and then, er, head off.

687
00:32:19,260 --> 00:32:20,650
Is that all you've got to say?

688
00:32:20,700 --> 00:32:24,740
Don't look at me. It's not my fault
and there is nothing I can do.

689
00:32:27,420 --> 00:32:29,580
Did they catch your nephew?

690
00:32:29,660 --> 00:32:30,850
- What?
- Your nephew.

691
00:32:30,900 --> 00:32:32,730
The one who was on the
run from the police.

692
00:32:32,780 --> 00:32:34,650
The one you sheltered here.

693
00:32:34,700 --> 00:32:37,260
Did they catch him?

694
00:32:37,340 --> 00:32:39,620
I don't believe so. No.

695
00:32:39,700 --> 00:32:43,740
I wonder if that was because
he was allowed to rest here.

696
00:32:43,820 --> 00:32:45,760
I think, maybe, it was.

697
00:32:46,060 --> 00:32:50,940
Septimus Spratt, if I am sacked,
I am taking you down with me.

698
00:32:51,060 --> 00:32:54,180
And my sin is not a criminal offence.

699
00:32:56,100 --> 00:32:57,940
What can I do?

700
00:32:58,020 --> 00:33:00,050
I can't talk to her before she's dressed.

701
00:33:00,100 --> 00:33:02,970
No. Well, while you're waiting, you
can think of what you're going to say.

702
00:33:03,020 --> 00:33:06,120
Unless you want to find
yourself sewing mail bags.

703
00:33:07,220 --> 00:33:09,930
I'm not promising anything. I mean,
suppose she doesn't listen to me?

704
00:33:09,980 --> 00:33:13,940
You'd better hope she does, Mr Spratt.

705
00:33:14,020 --> 00:33:17,060
You'd better hope she does.

706
00:33:19,460 --> 00:33:21,930
I think she likes him, yes. But
I don't believe she's serious.

707
00:33:21,980 --> 00:33:23,290
Why not?

708
00:33:23,340 --> 00:33:25,450
Lady Mary has quite a
sense of her own importance

709
00:33:25,500 --> 00:33:27,450
and I doubt he's enough for her.

710
00:33:27,500 --> 00:33:29,780
Besides, what would he do here?

711
00:33:29,860 --> 00:33:32,130
Hang about, testing his cars on the drive?

712
00:33:32,180 --> 00:33:33,490
He could always get a job.

713
00:33:33,540 --> 00:33:34,730
Doing what?

714
00:33:34,780 --> 00:33:38,780
I'm not convinced there's a big
demand for racing drivers in Thirsk.

715
00:33:38,860 --> 00:33:42,860
So, Lady Mary does not believe
that love conquers all?

716
00:33:42,940 --> 00:33:45,410
We mustn't be hard on
her. She is what she is.

717
00:33:45,460 --> 00:33:49,340
But I'd say she'll want someone who
brings just as much to the table.

718
00:33:49,420 --> 00:33:50,650
Maybe I'm wrong.

719
00:33:50,700 --> 00:33:53,090
I hope so. I'd like her to be happy.

720
00:33:53,140 --> 00:33:56,860
I'm happy, and I want
everyone to be happy.

721
00:33:56,940 --> 00:33:58,210
Are you really happy?

722
00:33:58,260 --> 00:34:00,090
I'm happier than I have ever been.

723
00:34:00,140 --> 00:34:03,820
Happy, impatient, excited.

724
00:34:03,900 --> 00:34:05,410
Don't say too much.

725
00:34:05,460 --> 00:34:06,850
It frightens me.

726
00:34:06,900 --> 00:34:08,810
We've still got months.

727
00:34:08,860 --> 00:34:10,170
Nothing will go wrong.

728
00:34:10,220 --> 00:34:12,010
Bad harvest, bad harvest.

729
00:34:12,060 --> 00:34:14,260
What does that mean?

730
00:34:14,340 --> 00:34:18,500
In the old days, when the crop was good,
the farmers used to shout "Bad harvest!",

731
00:34:18,580 --> 00:34:22,260
so the gods wouldn't grow jealous
of their luck and destroy them.

732
00:34:22,340 --> 00:34:23,900
Bad harvest!

733
00:34:25,140 --> 00:34:26,450
That ought to do it.

734
00:34:26,500 --> 00:34:28,420
Let's hope so.

735
00:34:28,500 --> 00:34:31,500
We'd better hurry or we'll
be late for breakfast.

736
00:34:32,940 --> 00:34:34,970
- Well? - She wants
you. In the drawing room.

737
00:34:35,020 --> 00:34:36,290
What did she say?

738
00:34:36,340 --> 00:34:37,410
What did you say?

739
00:34:37,460 --> 00:34:41,180
I told her your crime was
an excess of loyalty,

740
00:34:41,260 --> 00:34:44,140
that your devotion to
her had made you blind.

741
00:34:44,220 --> 00:34:48,020
It was the smugness of the
doctor that got my goat.

742
00:34:48,100 --> 00:34:49,770
That, I did not say.

743
00:34:49,820 --> 00:34:52,540
No, no. The point is, did it work?

744
00:34:52,620 --> 00:34:56,780
I reminded her how hard it is to
find properly trained staff now.

745
00:34:56,860 --> 00:34:58,090
Yeah. And did it work?

746
00:34:58,140 --> 00:35:00,530
I asked her how long it
had taken to break you in.

747
00:35:00,580 --> 00:35:02,330
We'll have less of that talk.

748
00:35:02,380 --> 00:35:04,900
I just want to know, did it work?

749
00:35:05,940 --> 00:35:08,540
She's going to give you one more chance.

750
00:35:09,580 --> 00:35:10,610
Hallelujah!

751
00:35:10,660 --> 00:35:13,090
But mind you, act surprised
when she tells you.

752
00:35:13,140 --> 00:35:16,860
I will rival Saint Paul
in my astonishment.

753
00:35:17,060 --> 00:35:18,330
Right.

754
00:35:18,380 --> 00:35:20,820
Well, you better get upstairs.

755
00:35:20,900 --> 00:35:22,010
Oh, and Miss Denker,

756
00:35:22,060 --> 00:35:26,860
I don't want to hear another
mention of my unfortunate nephew.

757
00:35:27,380 --> 00:35:29,580
Oh, well, that rather depends.

758
00:35:29,860 --> 00:35:31,690
- On what?
- On whether or not

759
00:35:31,740 --> 00:35:33,880
I need to mention him again.

760
00:35:42,180 --> 00:35:43,690
He's not arrived, then?

761
00:35:43,740 --> 00:35:45,210
No, not yet.

762
00:35:45,260 --> 00:35:49,180
But what we want to know is, by
what power you made him come here.

763
00:35:49,260 --> 00:35:51,130
Well, power of personality.

764
00:35:51,180 --> 00:35:52,730
I'm not sure it would've worked for me.

765
00:35:52,780 --> 00:35:55,580
Works for me. Are you quite well?

766
00:35:55,660 --> 00:35:58,660
I will be all right as long
as no one asks me how I am.

767
00:35:58,740 --> 00:36:01,450
You heard your note nearly
proved fatal to poor Denker?

768
00:36:01,500 --> 00:36:04,410
Well, I only intended that she
be ticked off, not beheaded.

769
00:36:04,460 --> 00:36:09,060
I gather it was Spratt who saved her,
which is a surprise in any number of ways.

770
00:36:09,140 --> 00:36:11,260
Mama, what are you doing?

771
00:36:11,420 --> 00:36:15,580
Carson, Carson, can you change these
place cards in the dining room?

772
00:36:15,660 --> 00:36:17,850
- I'm not sure her ladyship...
- Robert?

773
00:36:17,900 --> 00:36:20,940
- Just do it.
- Yes, very well, m'lord.

774
00:36:21,020 --> 00:36:22,730
You'll be in a very junior seat.

775
00:36:22,780 --> 00:36:25,340
Il faut reculer pour mieux sauter.

776
00:36:25,420 --> 00:36:26,610
I found my editor.

777
00:36:26,660 --> 00:36:27,770
And had some fun, too, I hope.

778
00:36:27,820 --> 00:36:31,060
I went dancing at the Cafe de Paris,
which felt very young and gay.

779
00:36:31,140 --> 00:36:33,530
And we saw Henry Talbot
try out a racing car.

780
00:36:33,580 --> 00:36:35,250
So, now we're all members
of the Bright Young Things.

781
00:36:35,300 --> 00:36:36,730
I don't know about "bright."

782
00:36:36,780 --> 00:36:38,610
The Right Honourable Neville Chamberlain,

783
00:36:38,660 --> 00:36:40,010
Minister for Health.

784
00:36:40,060 --> 00:36:42,700
Lord Grantham. Lady Grantham.

785
00:36:42,780 --> 00:36:46,700
Minister, how kind of you to find
time for us in your busy schedule.

786
00:36:46,780 --> 00:36:48,330
We're very grateful.

787
00:36:48,380 --> 00:36:50,690
I wouldn't have the courage
to refuse your mother-in-law.

788
00:36:50,740 --> 00:36:52,340
There you are.

789
00:36:52,420 --> 00:36:54,220
How is dear Anne?

790
00:36:54,300 --> 00:36:55,690
She sends her greetings.

791
00:36:55,740 --> 00:36:58,140
Oh, how grown up you all are.

792
00:36:58,220 --> 00:37:04,460
You know, I remember so well when
you and she were young and carefree,

793
00:37:04,540 --> 00:37:06,970
looking for fun wherever
you could find it.

794
00:37:07,020 --> 00:37:08,450
I know you do.

795
00:37:08,500 --> 00:37:13,420
Yes, well, but I always say,
let the past stay in the past.

796
00:37:13,500 --> 00:37:15,440
I always say that, too.

797
00:37:17,060 --> 00:37:19,900
Have you met my cousin Mrs Crawley?

798
00:37:19,980 --> 00:37:21,690
No, indeed. It's a great honour.

799
00:37:21,740 --> 00:37:25,540
I gather you're here to discuss the
new plans for managing our health.

800
00:37:25,620 --> 00:37:29,380
I know I'm here to discuss a topic
Lady Grantham is interested in.

801
00:37:29,460 --> 00:37:32,200
Excuse me, would you
care for one of these?

802
00:37:34,020 --> 00:37:35,170
Thank you.

803
00:37:35,220 --> 00:37:37,170
I thought you needed rescuing.

804
00:37:37,220 --> 00:37:39,770
Our own scrapes are bad enough without
being dragged into other people's.

805
00:37:39,820 --> 00:37:41,210
How well you understand me.

806
00:37:41,260 --> 00:37:42,970
I'm afraid you're in for
some rigorous debate.

807
00:37:43,020 --> 00:37:44,690
I wish I weren't.

808
00:37:44,900 --> 00:37:46,500
Shall we go in?

809
00:37:46,980 --> 00:37:51,220
We don't want to wear the minister out
before he's even had a chance to sit down.

810
00:37:52,980 --> 00:37:55,530
She can't protect him in the dining room.

811
00:37:55,580 --> 00:37:58,980
You'll stop at nothing to get
your own way. Isn't that the truth?

812
00:37:59,060 --> 00:38:00,500
Indeed.

813
00:38:00,580 --> 00:38:06,740
It is a quality I share with Marlborough,
Wellington and my late mother.

814
00:38:06,820 --> 00:38:10,180
I was trained in a hard school

815
00:38:10,260 --> 00:38:12,700
and I fight accordingly.

816
00:38:12,780 --> 00:38:16,780
No, he's a coming man. I've read
he may be prime minister one day.

817
00:38:16,860 --> 00:38:18,610
Well, he's no friend of the unions.

818
00:38:18,660 --> 00:38:20,490
For which he is to be congratulated.

819
00:38:20,540 --> 00:38:22,610
Is the consomme ready, Mrs Patmore?

820
00:38:22,660 --> 00:38:24,860
We should get out of your way.

821
00:38:26,260 --> 00:38:28,600
You never said how the trial went.

822
00:38:28,660 --> 00:38:30,210
I didn't have to testify in the end.

823
00:38:30,260 --> 00:38:33,300
He looked at the list of
witnesses and changed his plea.

824
00:38:33,380 --> 00:38:35,890
You must have a sense
of unfinished business.

825
00:38:35,940 --> 00:38:37,770
That's clever of you.

826
00:38:37,820 --> 00:38:41,620
I won't ask what it was, but I
hope you can leave it behind.

827
00:38:41,740 --> 00:38:43,690
I'm not sure. But thank you.

828
00:38:43,740 --> 00:38:46,820
Mr Molesley says the Dowager's
got the bit between her teeth.

829
00:38:46,900 --> 00:38:49,130
Then I don't give much for
Mr Chamberlain's chances.

830
00:38:49,180 --> 00:38:52,180
- Nor me. - But surely, if
it's important to the area,

831
00:38:52,260 --> 00:38:53,970
he'll want to listen?

832
00:38:54,020 --> 00:38:56,360
He'll just want to get out alive.

833
00:38:57,660 --> 00:39:00,610
The system has worked well
here for a hundred years!

834
00:39:00,660 --> 00:39:04,660
Why must we destroy everything in our
path simply for the sake of change?

835
00:39:04,740 --> 00:39:07,780
I'm not sure that's a true
representation of the case.

836
00:39:07,860 --> 00:39:10,610
Exactly. There are many
benefits to be had from the plan.

837
00:39:10,660 --> 00:39:12,090
But benefits for whom?

838
00:39:12,140 --> 00:39:13,250
Goodness.

839
00:39:13,300 --> 00:39:16,460
I thought I was here to be
lectured by a united group,

840
00:39:16,540 --> 00:39:18,210
not to witness a battle royal.

841
00:39:18,260 --> 00:39:20,600
Oh! Don't you enjoy a good fight?

842
00:39:20,660 --> 00:39:21,970
I'm not sure I do, really.

843
00:39:22,020 --> 00:39:25,940
My mother-in-law has a certain myopia when
it comes to anyone else's point of view.

844
00:39:26,020 --> 00:39:28,730
On the contrary, I have
a clarity of vision

845
00:39:28,780 --> 00:39:32,660
that allows me to resist a
housemaid's trap of sentimentality.

846
00:39:32,740 --> 00:39:34,970
Your enthusiasm is getting
the better of your manners.

847
00:39:35,020 --> 00:39:36,770
Can't we stop this beastly row?

848
00:39:36,820 --> 00:39:38,170
How I wish we could.

849
00:39:38,220 --> 00:39:39,330
Because I...

850
00:39:39,380 --> 00:39:40,780
I...

851
00:39:40,860 --> 00:39:42,460
I'm so sorry...

852
00:39:48,860 --> 00:39:50,490
Thomas, on his left side.

853
00:39:50,540 --> 00:39:51,970
Give me napkins!

854
00:39:52,020 --> 00:39:53,290
I'll call the ambulance.

855
00:39:53,340 --> 00:39:54,890
- Keep him warm.
- Take my coat.

856
00:39:54,940 --> 00:39:56,730
- What is it?
- His ulcer has burst.

857
00:39:56,780 --> 00:39:58,130
- What?
- Will he be all right?

858
00:39:58,180 --> 00:40:00,210
We must get him to hospital
as quickly as we can.

859
00:40:00,260 --> 00:40:02,740
I'm here, darling, don't worry.

860
00:40:02,820 --> 00:40:06,940
If this is it, just know I
have loved you very, very much.

861
00:40:07,020 --> 00:40:09,650
This isn't it, darling.
Don't... We won't let this be it.

862
00:40:09,700 --> 00:40:11,900
- Some water?
- No! No water.

863
00:40:12,780 --> 00:40:15,780
Just keep him steady until
the ambulance arrives.

864
00:40:28,460 --> 00:40:29,530
Is he very bad?

865
00:40:29,580 --> 00:40:31,610
I've rung for the ambulance.
It'll be here at any moment.

866
00:40:31,660 --> 00:40:34,620
Miss Baxter, Anna, fetch
their ladyships' coats.

867
00:40:34,700 --> 00:40:36,850
- Don't forget Lady Edith.
- What about me, Mr Carson?

868
00:40:36,900 --> 00:40:39,370
You might put together some
things that could be useful.

869
00:40:39,420 --> 00:40:41,730
But hurry, there's no time to lose.

870
00:40:41,780 --> 00:40:44,340
I can't believe it.

871
00:40:44,420 --> 00:40:47,820
Life is short, death is sure.

872
00:40:47,900 --> 00:40:49,700
That is all we know.

873
00:40:51,780 --> 00:40:54,690
There is a man who's been
shaken to the roots of his soul.

874
00:40:54,740 --> 00:40:58,140
Everything he based his life
on has proved mortal, after all.

875
00:40:58,220 --> 00:41:00,730
We've no time for philosophy, Mrs Patmore.

876
00:41:00,780 --> 00:41:02,250
What can we do to help?

877
00:41:02,300 --> 00:41:04,370
Let's send up some coffee.

878
00:41:11,900 --> 00:41:14,610
Lady Grantham, I'm not sure
how much use I can be here.

879
00:41:14,660 --> 00:41:16,250
Of course, you should go.

880
00:41:16,300 --> 00:41:17,730
I will consider the new plan.

881
00:41:17,780 --> 00:41:20,180
Don't. That is, let it stand.

882
00:41:20,260 --> 00:41:23,250
I believe the change will work
well for both establishments.

883
00:41:23,300 --> 00:41:24,610
Very well.

884
00:41:24,660 --> 00:41:26,770
- Lady Grantham.
- Give my love to dear Annie.

885
00:41:26,820 --> 00:41:28,090
I will.

886
00:41:28,620 --> 00:41:30,490
The ambulance is here!

887
00:41:31,100 --> 00:41:34,210
Don't reprimand me, Mama, I think
the new system will be better

888
00:41:34,260 --> 00:41:36,290
and I haven't got time to be diplomatic.

889
00:41:36,340 --> 00:41:38,930
Don't you think I have
enough things to worry about?

890
00:41:38,980 --> 00:41:41,170
It's better we should be honest.
There've been too many secrets.

891
00:41:41,220 --> 00:41:43,700
Let's have no more of them.

892
00:41:43,780 --> 00:41:47,260
If you mean Marigold, that's
settled and you know I am sorry.

893
00:41:47,340 --> 00:41:49,330
Now let us concentrate on Robert?

894
00:41:49,380 --> 00:41:52,050
They're ready. They want to take him now.

895
00:41:53,060 --> 00:41:55,460
Girls. Mary. Edith.

896
00:41:55,540 --> 00:41:57,010
We must go!

897
00:42:00,900 --> 00:42:04,300
Edith, dear, telephone with any news.

898
00:42:04,380 --> 00:42:06,650
- No matter how late.
- I will.

899
00:42:07,780 --> 00:42:10,610
I'll take you home in my
car. And Lady Grantham.

900
00:42:10,660 --> 00:42:12,130
In a moment.

901
00:42:12,940 --> 00:42:14,290
You're quite pale.

902
00:42:14,340 --> 00:42:16,010
We'll go together.

903
00:42:16,060 --> 00:42:17,530
You've had a shock.

904
00:42:17,580 --> 00:42:20,500
I think I've had a few
shocks this evening.

905
00:42:30,700 --> 00:42:34,660
- They're bringing your car round.
- Oh, let the ambulance get away.

906
00:42:36,980 --> 00:42:39,690
There's a dinner that delivered
more than you bargained for.

907
00:42:39,740 --> 00:42:41,530
Are you going to follow them?

908
00:42:41,580 --> 00:42:44,330
I don't want to crowd him, but
they'll ring when they have news.

909
00:42:44,380 --> 00:42:46,010
Can you let my office know?

910
00:42:46,060 --> 00:42:47,460
Of course.

911
00:42:48,020 --> 00:42:50,490
If you'll do me a favour
and answer one question.

912
00:42:50,540 --> 00:42:52,530
Why did you come here tonight?

913
00:42:52,580 --> 00:42:55,540
Your grandmother-in-law
can be very persuasive.

914
00:42:55,620 --> 00:42:57,610
I'd love to know how she did it.

915
00:42:57,660 --> 00:43:00,210
My wife has a brother
called Horace de Vere Cole.

916
00:43:00,260 --> 00:43:03,010
- You may have heard of him.
- The prankster?

917
00:43:03,060 --> 00:43:06,220
Didn't he board a warship pretending to
be the leader of a Turkish delegation?

918
00:43:06,300 --> 00:43:07,930
Abyssinian, but yes.

919
00:43:07,980 --> 00:43:10,330
He was always doing that sort of thing.

920
00:43:10,380 --> 00:43:14,780
Some years ago, he and a few friends
dug a trench across Piccadilly

921
00:43:14,860 --> 00:43:17,400
and London was thrown into total chaos.

922
00:43:17,460 --> 00:43:20,540
- But what --
- I was one of the chaps responsible.

923
00:43:20,620 --> 00:43:22,490
We dressed as workmen
and no one stopped us.

924
00:43:22,540 --> 00:43:25,010
And by the time we'd finished
every road was jammed

925
00:43:25,060 --> 00:43:27,330
from the East End to Belgrave Square.

926
00:43:27,380 --> 00:43:29,410
And old Lady Grantham
threatened to give you away?

927
00:43:29,460 --> 00:43:31,460
It was long ago now,

928
00:43:31,540 --> 00:43:35,020
but the papers would be sure to
make it look as bad as possible

929
00:43:35,100 --> 00:43:38,740
and a dinner seemed a price
worth paying to avert it.

930
00:43:38,820 --> 00:43:41,020
And there's my car.

931
00:43:41,100 --> 00:43:43,580
So, I'd better be off.

932
00:44:25,860 --> 00:44:27,740
Thank you, m'lady.

933
00:44:35,540 --> 00:44:37,500
That was Lady Mary.

934
00:44:37,580 --> 00:44:41,620
His lordship's had his
operation and now he's resting.

935
00:44:41,700 --> 00:44:43,490
Her ladyship is staying overnight,

936
00:44:43,540 --> 00:44:45,970
but Lady Mary and Lady
Edith will come home.

937
00:44:46,020 --> 00:44:49,420
- What was the operation?
- They performed a gastrectomy.

938
00:44:49,500 --> 00:44:50,970
What's that?

939
00:44:51,620 --> 00:44:53,210
No business of ours.

940
00:44:53,260 --> 00:44:55,090
Will he be all right?

941
00:44:55,140 --> 00:44:57,330
It sounds as if he has a good chance.

942
00:44:57,380 --> 00:44:59,020
Thank God.

943
00:44:59,100 --> 00:45:01,170
I'll find out if there's
anything he needs in the morning.

944
00:45:01,220 --> 00:45:03,450
I'll walk down there now with
some things for her ladyship.

945
00:45:03,500 --> 00:45:05,210
I'll come with you.

946
00:45:05,260 --> 00:45:06,850
I'm quite relieved.

947
00:45:06,900 --> 00:45:07,930
Of course you are.

948
00:45:07,980 --> 00:45:10,980
No, I didn't think I'd mind one
way or the other, to be honest.

949
00:45:11,060 --> 00:45:14,220
I must be getting soft in my old age.

950
00:45:14,300 --> 00:45:16,890
Well, don't let the other animals
find out, or they'll pounce.

951
00:45:16,940 --> 00:45:19,460
Leave him alone. Now, we should go.

952
00:45:20,980 --> 00:45:24,220
We can take it in turns tomorrow.
So Mama can have some rest.

953
00:45:24,300 --> 00:45:25,570
Very well.

954
00:45:25,620 --> 00:45:29,700
I'll go first. Stark can take
me there and bring Mama back.

955
00:45:29,780 --> 00:45:31,570
What a terrifying reminder.

956
00:45:31,620 --> 00:45:33,850
In one second your whole life can change.

957
00:45:33,900 --> 00:45:35,040
Yes.

958
00:45:35,100 --> 00:45:38,660
It only takes a moment for
everything to feel quite different.

959
00:45:38,740 --> 00:45:40,890
I'm going to check on the children.

960
00:45:40,940 --> 00:45:42,680
Of course you are.

961
00:45:43,460 --> 00:45:44,930
Good night.

962
00:45:46,140 --> 00:45:47,490
How is he?

963
00:45:47,540 --> 00:45:50,050
He'll be all right, but it's
knocked the stuffing out of him.

964
00:45:50,100 --> 00:45:52,610
We'll have to make sure the load
is lightened when he comes home.

965
00:45:52,660 --> 00:45:54,600
To be more precise, Tom,

966
00:45:54,660 --> 00:45:58,540
from now on you and I need to take
full responsibility for the estate.

967
00:45:58,620 --> 00:46:01,090
We'll involve him in the
big decisions of course,

968
00:46:01,140 --> 00:46:03,290
but he mustn't have any more worry.

969
00:46:03,340 --> 00:46:05,850
It's why he got the
ulcer in the first place.

970
00:46:05,900 --> 00:46:08,170
So long live our own Queen Mary.

971
00:46:08,900 --> 00:46:10,370
Good night.

972
00:46:18,460 --> 00:46:21,290
Mr Carson told us his
lordship is going to recover.

973
00:46:21,340 --> 00:46:22,730
They seem to think so.

974
00:46:22,780 --> 00:46:24,610
Everyone was very happy to hear it.

975
00:46:24,660 --> 00:46:26,450
Can I fetch you anything?

976
00:46:26,500 --> 00:46:28,820
I just want to go to bed.

977
00:46:28,900 --> 00:46:31,980
- You must be absolutely
exhausted. - Hmm.

978
00:46:33,700 --> 00:46:36,660
Anna, can I ask you something?

979
00:46:36,740 --> 00:46:40,220
Is there any talk in the servants'
hall about Miss Marigold?

980
00:46:41,380 --> 00:46:43,450
What sort of talk, m'lady?

981
00:46:44,500 --> 00:46:48,460
Well, everyone thinks she's a lucky
little girl, being taken in by the family.

982
00:46:48,540 --> 00:46:50,610
But they would think that.

983
00:46:52,500 --> 00:46:54,770
And that's all you want to say?

984
00:46:55,180 --> 00:46:57,860
Why? What else should I say?

985
00:46:57,940 --> 00:46:59,660
Never mind.

986
00:46:59,740 --> 00:47:02,420
I'm too tired to talk any more tonight.

987
00:47:14,620 --> 00:47:17,770
We are opening the house for one day
for charity and there is none to it.

988
00:47:17,820 --> 00:47:20,410
What are they paying to see?
We have nothing to show them.

989
